


  
Experiment 5 — Docker: Architecture, Container Life Cycle and Docker Commands

Step 1 — Install Docker Desktop
Download Docker Desktop for Windows and complete the installation.

Step 2 — Check Docker context
docker context ls

Step 3 — Select Docker Desktop context
docker context use desktop-linux

Step 4 — Check running containers
docker ps

Step 5 — Check Docker version
docker --version

Step 6 — Display Docker information
docker info

Step 7 — Download Nginx image
docker pull nginx

Step 8 — List Docker images
docker images

Step 9 — Remove Nginx image
docker rmi nginx

Step 10 — Download Nginx image again
docker pull nginx

Step 11 — Run Hello World container
docker run hello-world

Step 12 — Run Alpine container interactively
docker run -it alpine sh

Step 13 — Display kernel information
uname -a

Step 14 — Display Alpine information
cat /etc/os-release

Step 15 — Check working directory
pwd

Step 16 — List files
ls -l

Step 17 — Exit the container
exit

Step 18 — Display command history
Get-History



Experiment 3 — Git: Install, Configure and Execute Git Operations

Step 1 — Update the system
sudo apt update

Step 2 — Install Git
sudo apt install git -y
git --version

Step 3 — Configure Git
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
git config --list

Step 4 — Create a working directory
mkdir GitDemo
cd GitDemo

Step 5 — Initialize a Git repository
git init

Step 6 — Create a file
touch file1.txt
cat file1.txt

Step 7 — Check repository status
git status

Step 8 — Add the file to the staging area
git add file1.txt

Step 9 — Commit the change
git commit -m "First Commit"

Step 10 — Create a GitHub repository
Open GitHub → Login/Create account → New Repository → Enter repository name → Create repository

Step 11 — Connect local repository to GitHub
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/GitDemo.git
git remote -v

Step 12 — Rename the default branch
git branch -M main

Step 13 — Push the repository to GitHub
git push -u origin main
